/-!
# Uniqueness of connected immersed subgroups

The underlying subgroup of a connected immersed Lie subgroup is generated by exponentials of
its differential range. Inclusion of differential ranges yields a unique smooth homomorphism
between the presenting Lie groups commuting with their ambient injections. Equality yields a
canonical smooth group equivalence.

These maps give the uniqueness and morphism statements for the subalgebra–immersed-subgroup
correspondence. Each source retains its intrinsic topology, and neither image is assumed closed.

## Main results

* `le_of_range_lieMapToHom_le`: inclusion of differential ranges implies inclusion of images.
* `eq_of_range_lieMapToHom_eq`: equal differential ranges give equal images.
* `contMDiff_homOfLe`: differential-range inclusion makes the canonical factorization smooth.
* `existsUnique_contMDiff_monoidHom_of_range_lieMapToHom_le`: the unique smooth factorization.
* `contMDiffHomOfRangeLe`: the bundled smooth factorization, with differential, identity
  and composition formulas.
* `contMDiff_mulEquivOfEq` and `contMDiff_mulEquivOfEq_symm`: the canonical equivalence and
  its inverse are smooth when the differential ranges agree.
* `diffeomorphOfRangeEq`: the resulting diffeomorphism, with multiplication and inverse formulas.

The intrinsic presenting Lie groups are uniquely smoothly isomorphic by a group equivalence
commuting with their ambient injections. Exponential naturality determines this equivalence in
local exponential coordinates, hence makes both directions smooth.

## References

* J. M. Lee, *Introduction to Smooth Manifolds*, 2nd ed., Springer (2013), Theorem 19.26,
  pp.506–507, and Proposition 7.17, pp.157–158.
-/

public section

